  "updated_at": "2025-02-04T11:44:57Z",
  "body": "Great, that makes a lot of sense and I think I have got my head around most of the code - although the original code does lack some ascii art depicting how the memory is allocated >.< so the pointer arithmetic is a little hard to follow.\n\nI may go and tackle the potentially more elegant (and harder to debug) solution at some point - but it's good to know that this works (and what its limitations are).\n\nI note that the original decision for not supporting this was that it \"wasted memory\" and was out of spec.  I could imagine ~if the original code didn't zero all the memory,~ they could use anonymous mmap to pre-allocate the static area in pages so it wouldn't occupy any space until the pages were touched (unless they were also considering support for embedded systems without MMUs _mmmm_).\n\nEdit: Anonymous maps are zero'd - so an implementation with anonymous maps is simpler than I thought: I think it's possible to allocate a large chunk up front, at least a few pages worth without any significant overhead.",
  "author_association": "NONE",
  "pin": null,
